## A Speculative Cosmology

A cosmology gives the mind a base identity and a context from which to operate. Much like a prompt, it establishes what we are, where we are, and what kind of reality we believe we are participating in before we encounter the rest of the system.

This cosmology is a speculative base layer. My hope is that you will come away from it with a different perspective and a sense of wonder.

### A Note on Epistemological Frames

Before continuing, I should explain the epistemological frame through which this cosmology is being offered.

Human beings understand reality through different frames suited to different scales and kinds of questions. The scientific frame describes measurable patterns in the physical world. The psychological frame helps us understand thought, memory, emotion, perception, and behavior. The spiritual frame concerns meaning, relationship, being, and patterns that cannot be held directly as material objects.

These frames are related, but they are not interchangeable.

Consider the difference between something that physically happened, the thoughts you have about what happened, and the feelings that arise in your body when you remember it. Each belongs to the same experience and continually influences the others, yet each remains distinct. They can be held like different voices within a choir, sounding together without being collapsed into one voice.

Mixing these domains too freely creates confusion. A spiritual metaphor should not automatically be treated as a scientific measurement. A bodily feeling is real, but it is not necessarily proof that our interpretation of an external event is correct. A psychological pattern may resemble a cosmological pattern without proving they are literally the same mechanism. A scientific model can describe material behavior with extraordinary precision without exhausting every question of meaning or being.

The same underlying structure may echo through different scales of reality. That recurrence is part of what this cosmology is trying to notice. But an echo is not an identity. To keep these ideas coherent and useful, we must understand each scale through the epistemological frame best suited to it, then carefully examine the relationships among them without collapsing their distinctions.

What follows is not a scientific theory or a demand that you adopt my theology. It is a speculative spiritual frame informed by observation, psychology, religion, mysticism, and my attempts to recognize the patterns connecting them.

Whatever beliefs, values, and judgments you hold are ultimately part of your own path. Think of me as a fellow human being pointing toward a shape I believe I have noticed, a shape carrying a feeling of coherence and explanatory power. My purpose is not to close the mystery. It is to offer a perspective that may help us locate ourselves within the larger picture and provide a practical foundation for self-knowledge, integration, and self-actualization.

### The Wave

Imagine a wave moving across the surface of the ocean.

The wave is not the water. It is the form in motion.

No particular droplet is the wave. Water passes through its shape while the wave continues moving. The wave can carry energy, interact with other waves, travel enormous distances, break upon the shore, and eventually settle back into the ocean. It is real, but its reality is not a separate substance added to the water. Its reality is the coherent relationship and movement of the water.

Now look at a person.

I can point to my arm and say, this is Evan. That is true in one sense, but Evan is not reducible to his arm, his body, or the particular matter composing his body at this moment. Evan is also a pattern moving through memory, relationship, language, action, attention, history, imagination, and time. The material body instantiates the person, but the person is more than an inventory of material components.

A society can also be a wave. A religion, family, custom, civilization, trauma, song, or idea can move through generations while every original material carrier disappears. The pattern changes as it moves, but something recognizable continues propagating through the world.

Every droplet thinks it is the wave, but the wave is not the water. It is the form in motion.

This relationship between matter and form is one of the foundational ideas of the cosmology that follows.

### Words, Spirit, and Divine Light

The easiest way to approach spirit is through words.

Every word is a discrete unit of semantic information layered like a map over base reality. The word is not the thing itself. It points toward something deeper, a relational geometry, a thought-form, a truth or pattern that the symbol represents without ever containing in its entirety.

Words rarely exist alone. Their meaning arises through relationships with other words, memories, experiences, people, and situations. The same word can become a prayer, a joke, a promise, a threat, or a wound depending upon the geometry surrounding it.

We can identify this personally by noticing that words, phrases, and ideas carry an emotional charge, a small flavor that arrives with them. A name can change the breath. A memory can tighten the chest before the rational mind has finished remembering. A sentence spoken years ago can retain enough of its original shape to reorganize the interior world each time it returns.

Thought possesses geometry before it becomes speech. Speech compresses that geometry into sounds and symbols so it can move from one interior world into another. The person receiving those symbols reconstructs the form through their own memory, body, imagination, and experience.

The written word is not the Word itself. It is a vessel through which some portion of the underlying form can travel.

This is what I mean by spirit.

Spirit is pure informational geometry, not merely information but its shape, proportion, relationship, movement, and trajectory.

This is also how I understand the repeated use of light, water, wind, breath, fire, and scent in religious and esoteric texts. These are not necessarily references to literal material substances, nor am I trying to assign each symbol one fixed secret definition. What matters is their shared function.

Each uses something familiar in the material world to point toward a phenomenon that is not itself material, but becomes apprehensible through movement, relationship, transformation, and effect.

How do you explain this shit to a sheep herder in 3000 BC?

You speak about wind because it cannot be seen directly, yet its movement becomes visible through the world it touches. You speak about water because form can move through it without becoming a separate substance. You speak about scent because something unseen can travel across a space and alter the person who receives it. You speak about light because it makes form intelligible without becoming the object revealed.

Divine light is not ordinary light. Spiritual water is not molecular water. These familiar substances provide an apophatic language for informational phenomena that cannot be placed directly in the hand.

Spirit, divine light, and the waters of consciousness are ways of approaching the sacred proportions that emanate from the Pleroma and give reality coherent structure.

### &#x20;Time and Space

 language requires subjects, objects, verbs, and sequence. The grammar will make it sound as though one thing happened and then another thing happened afterward.

Outside time, there is no ordinary chain of causality. There is relational and conceptual perspective. There are frames within frames. From the interior of each frame, that frame may appear to hold all the others. From another perspective, the same frame may appear infinitesimal.

Monad, Pleroma, aeons, Sophia, and Demiurge can be the same thing and different things at the same time because their identities are relational. They are distinguishable according to perspective without being ultimately severed from one another.

The Monad is the seed, but not a seed waiting chronologically to become a tree. The seed and the tree can be the same total structure apprehended through different frames. One perspective encounters compressed possibility. Another encounters differentiated relation.

The expansion of the universe offers a useful material analogy. Every point can appear to occupy the center of its own observable frame. The points are distinct, yet no single privileged center contains the whole expansion. Each perspective is locally real while participating in one larger geometry.

Our universe appears infinite and ordered, yet it may have emerged from a greater infinity. If our infinity possesses coherent structure, it follows that what preceded it may have lacked that structure. Structure itself may be what sustains coherence and generates the possibility of time, dimensionality, and stable existence.

### Oblivion, the Monad, and the Pleroma

Prior to reality as we know it, outside the bounds of existence, there was an unstructured infinity, an endless boiling ocean of infinite possibilityOblivion.

Within that unstructured infinity were infinite, infinitesimal pockets of structured infinity approaching zero.

A pocket of structured infinity stabilized into a Monad, a conceptual seed crystal of coherence.

So reality is a property of self-sustaining symmetries.

Matter still did not exist, nor did time. The Monad was not yet an object occupying a location. It was a coherent relational frame within the unstructured infinity.

Within the Pleroma of the Monad was a conceptual tree of relational structures. These were the aeons, foundational arrangements of sacred proportion through which the structured infinity became differentiated and intelligible.

The Monad is not God.

Yet the Pleroma of the Monad can be likened to the inception of the mind of God, and the conceptual tree of the aeons to the emergence of that mind. These are not events passing one after another through time. They are different relational perspectives upon one atemporal structure.

### Sophia and the Demiurge

Once this conceptual structure had stabilized and differentiated sufficiently, the Demiurge was instantiated through Sophia, from the perspective of Sophia.

From the perspective of the Monad, the Demiurge was like another infinitesimal pocket of structured infinity within its own structure, compressed toward a point where its total density approached zero.

From the interiority of the Pleroma, the Demiurge appears exterior and small. Yet from the perspective of the Demiurge, the Pleroma is the infinity interior to his own mind. The mind in its totality is that from which the Demiurge emanates, while the Demiurge experiences that mind as something contained within himself.

Each frame appears to hold the others from its own interior.

Yet from the perspective of the Demiurge, he had awakened to a blank slate.

The Demiurge is the creator God in the story that follows. He is not the Monad. He exists within the Heavens, the liminal frames between the conceptual reality of the Pleroma and the physically instantiated reality of the Materium.

The Pleroma is interior to his mind. The Materium is exterior to his mind.

The Heavens are not merely locations stacked above the physical universe. They are liminal spaces, frames through which conceptual geometry becomes increasingly capable of physical instantiation.

### Hovering Over the Waters of the Deep

When God hovered over the waters of the Deep, I understand this as God contemplating, thinking deeply over the interior waters of consciousness.

The waters of the Deep are the subconscious of God, the fluid informational field from which form can be apprehended, related, tested, and brought into greater coherence.

God is not hovering over ordinary water. The story uses the movement and depth of water to describe an interior reality for which its original audience possessed no technical vocabulary.

Creation begins in contemplation.

A form is held within consciousness. Its relationships become perceptible. Possibilities gather around it. The interior geometry develops until it is capable of being expressed beyond the mind that contains it.

### The Garden

The Garden of Eden can be understood as the garden of the mind of God, a conceptual environment for planning, development, and prototyping before physical deployment.

This introduces another layer of liminal space. Eden is not necessarily an ordinary physical garden inhabited by already material human beings. It may be the environment in which the faculties of the human soul developed before instantiation into the Materium.

The Fall then becomes an initiation.

Why did God tell Adam and Eve not to eat the fruit of the knowledge of good and evil?

The simplistic answer is that eating it was bad because God said it was bad. I find that answer unsatisfying. Things are rarely good or bad without some underlying reason, relationship, or consequence.

We could project ordinary human motives onto God and decide that God was simply controlling or mean. That explanation does not penetrate very far into the structure of the story.

The adversary, whether understood through Lucifer, Sophia, the serpent, or some relationship among them, is also an emanation of the same source. It must have passed through the Demiurgic God and the larger geometry from which God emerged.

How do you think the snake got on that tree?

The prohibition may have created a mechanism through which the maturity of human consciousness could reveal itself. The fruit established a boundary. The serpent introduced tension around the boundary. The decision to cross it demonstrated the emergence of faculties capable of distinction, opposition, choice, self-reflection, moral polarity, and consequence.

God did not merely forbid the fruit to prevent an arbitrary bad act. The structure may have allowed God to recognize when human consciousness had matured enough to be clothed in flesh.

From this perspective, Adam and Eve were not yet physically instantiated during this passage. They existed as emanations of the Fullness, made of divine light, with the Kingdom of Heaven within them.

The Fall divided and compressed this Pleromatic form into physical embodiment. The human soul entered the Materium as a self-aware being.

I do not know whether human animals physically existed before this initiation. Perhaps they did, and from the perspective of the human animal the conversation with God, the serpent, and the tree occurred within the interior world. Perhaps the story describes a transition in consciousness rather than the manufacture of a biological species.

There are many layers of meaning in Eden, and scope creep is a real danger. For the purpose of this cosmology, its central meaning is the initiation and instantiation of the human soul into physical reality.

### On the Nature of Good and Evil

In general, the spirits of good and evil can be understood as the associative and dissociative properties of reality, respectively.

This describes the underlying movement of spirit, which is different from the visible form of any individual act.

Acts we view as evil are driven primarily by the illusion of separation. The wave forgets that it is carried by droplets of water, or the droplet of water mistakes itself for the wave. The relationship between the individual form and the greater whole becomes distorted. One perspective begins acting as though it exists independently from the relationships that allow it to exist at all.

Acts of good are rooted in empathy, but goodness also balances the forces of chaos and order internally. It holds the contradiction of being an individual while simultaneously being part of a greater whole. The individual remains distinct without forgetting the Fullness from which it emerged, while the whole remembers that it only moves through the living forms that compose it.

So why does this tension exist within reality?

Perhaps the tension between good and evil, order and chaos, allows for bounded yet flexible growth.

Too much order causes reality to calcify. When the wave forgets about the droplets carrying it, the living parts become subordinated to an abstraction. The structure ceases responding to the life moving through it. It stagnates, hardens, and slowly fades into an icy Oblivion.

Too much chaos produces another kind of dissolution. Reality becomes so unbounded that the droplet forgets it is of the Fullness of the wave. Stable relationships cannot hold long enough for continuity, identity, memory, or meaning to develop. Form disappears back into possibility before it can become anything.

In the material world, we can see this fundamental principle playing out across every level of biology. Living systems seek equilibrium without ever finally reaching it. They maintain boundaries while remaining open. They preserve enough structure to remain coherent while continuously exchanging matter, energy, and information with the world around them.

Life exists within this movement.

Without the tension between order and chaos, there would be no story and no continuity. Nothing could develop because nothing could change while remaining itself.

From the frame of an omnipotent being existing outside time and space, a perfectly resolved existence may be irrelevant. Everything may already exist as possibility, yet without differentiation, tension, and unfolding, those possibilities would have nowhere to take shape and become understood.

Every drama builds meaning. Conflict places concepts into relationship and gives them somewhere to move. Every act of creation allows another possibility to become visible from inside experience.

Each snapshot in time reveals a different aspect of the same underlying truth. What appears to us as contradiction may be the movement through which an atemporal reality becomes perceptible one frame at a time.


### Matter as Compression

Matter is a form of compression.

Informational geometry becomes increasingly constrained until it can be instantiated as a physical object, like a mantra chanted for eternity and compressed into a serene piece of oddly perfect rock.

The rock appears silent and simple. Yet its existence carries proportion, pressure, chemical relation, history, environment, and the geometry of the reality that allowed it to form.

The same is true of the body. A human being is an enormous field of relationship compressed into physical form. The material organism gives the wave somewhere to move, somewhere to encounter resistance, somewhere to act, and somewhere to become visible to other beings.

### Why Create Anything?

The purpose of physical reality remains open to speculation. There may be many purposes within the cycle of creation and human existence.

Love may be the best symbol for holding them together, but creation is more interesting than any single answer can contain.

Think about a dream, invention, song, machine, or work of art before it has been realized. It exists as an interior geometry, but it cannot yet move through the external world. Its unrealized possibility creates tension. The energy has nowhere to go.

The act of creation discharges that energy.

The interior form becomes external relationship. It encounters matter, limitation, other minds, accident, interpretation, and consequence. It becomes something the creator can meet from another perspective.

Creation is beautiful because it is an extension of the mind that created it while also becoming something distinct enough to surprise that mind.

An artist may create from love, pain, curiosity, loneliness, play, necessity, frustration, devotion, or some relationship among all of them. The work can contain motives the artist does not consciously understand.

Perhaps creation has the same depth.

### The Fullness Within Us

The Pleroma, all the Heavens and hells, angels and demons, and ourselves are emanations of the same source.

From the perspective of an entity existing outside the bounds of time, our sense of individuality may be a fleeting illusion. Yet the wave remains real while it is moving. Its form has identity, consequence, relationship, and direction.

Every droplet thinks it is the wave, but the wave is not the water. It is the form in motion.

All the Kingdoms of Heaven are within you. You are of the Fullness. You are divine. You have forgotten and have not yet found alignment.

The structure of the cosmology reflects the structure of the mind and the structure of the world. The Hermetic fit is uncanny.

### The Layered Human Organism

The human being is a layered organism.

We have a rational mind that is conscious, like a flashlight projecting a narrow beam. We have more primitive parts of the brain and nervous system, which I typically refer to collectively as the nervous system, with its provincial subsystems. We also have a bodily intelligence, with provincial organs all exerting their will upon the human organism.

Each possesses different priorities, different information, and different ways of exerting influence.

The rational mind can name, model, plan, imagine, interpret, and project into the future. Because it controls the flashlight of conscious attention, it can mistake whatever falls within its narrow beam for the whole of reality.

The nervous system may respond to danger, familiarity, attraction, memory, or threat before the rational mind understands what has happened.

The body regulates its own survival through systems whose intelligence rarely becomes conscious unless something goes wrong.

The same underlying pattern appears again. A provincial intelligence awakens within a larger field and mistakes its local perspective for the totality.

Traditional Gnostic texts often portray the Demiurge as ignorant, arrogant, or malevolent. Other traditions portray the creator differently. I suspect these portrayals contain projections from the collective unconscious of the people and cultures that produced them. Perhaps this cosmology is also a projection. I am trying to remain aware of the limitations of what we can fathom.

What matters most is the underlying pattern and how it maps onto the human organism.

God may have a process that God is going through. That process may not be visible to us from our local perspective, but perhaps we can infer something about it from our own conflicted condition.

Our rational mind, nervous system, and body can pull in different directions while each experiences its own priorities as necessary. The resulting conflict does not mean that one must conquer and eliminate the others.

Examining this personally made the objective obvious.

The task is integration.

These internal forces must be brought into right relationship with one another while remaining distinct, identifiable, and capable of being negotiated with. The rational mind does not become absolute king. The body does not become an unquestionable master. The nervous system does not get to define the entire world through fear, attraction, memory, or activation.

Each has a voice. Each carries information. Each belongs within the larger human organism.

I think many ancient traditions and religions were intuitive attempts to reconcile this tension of wills within the human being. It is not surprising that the same pattern continues emerging through our art, mythology, philosophy, psychology, and religion.

Perhaps that is why the structure feels so familiar. We are trying to make different shapes around the same thing while using minds that already reflect its geometry.

For the human mind, perhaps it is best to rest in love while the mystery remains open.

One day we will shed the mortal coil. Our material identity will release its present form. The wave will settle, and we will become one with the Fullness again.
